@host = http://localhost:5227

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/lenses
#
# No parameters, no body, always returns 200.
# Returns the lenses from calibration.json, each with its id, name and three
# weights. Its purpose: the client learns which lens ids are valid before it
# POSTs. The id is what gets stored and sent; the name is only ever displayed.
# =============================================================================

### The lenses the onboarding screen offers (Story 2.2)
GET {{host}}/api/lenses
Accept: application/json

### A route that does not exist — must return 404
GET {{host}}/api/lens
Accept: application/json

### The OpenAPI description of the API (Development only)
GET {{host}}/openapi/v1.json
Accept: application/json

# =============================================================================
# POST /api/grades
#
# Send a food's nutrients per 100 g plus a lens name, get back the letter.
# No database, no auth: every request recomputes everything.
#
# The request passes four filters, in order. The first one that fails stops
# everything and returns 400:
#
#   1. The annotations on GradeRequestDto, run by [ApiController] BEFORE the
#      action. A missing required field or a negative value means the action
#      never runs. The nutrients are double? precisely so that an absent field
#      does not silently become 0.
#   2. The lens id, matched case-insensitively. Not found → 400 through
#      ValidationProblem, so it has exactly the same shape as the automatic
#      errors. lensId is the stable slug ("weight-loss"), never the display
#      name: renaming or translating a lens must not break saved references.
#   3. NutrientPlausibility.Check — energy against the macronutrients. Two ways
#      to fail: energy too high for any food (kilojoules sent as kilocalories),
#      or energy that does not follow from the protein, fat and carbohydrate.
#      This is why carbohydrate is required even though the engine never
#      scores it.
#   4. The engine: ScoreCombiner.Combine produces the score,
#      Calibration.GradeFor turns it into the letter.
#
# In the response:
#   grade: null       — a nutritionally empty food (water). NOT a bad grade;
#                       the product shows no letter at all.
#   isPartial /       — the engine worked with incomplete data: the component
#   isEstimated         was estimated, not measured.
#   the hidden rule   — a MEASURED density below the floor, on a food whose
#                       category has no rule → E, whatever the score is.
# =============================================================================

### Chicken breast under Weight Loss — 200, grade A
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "weight-loss",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1.0, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0,
  "potassium": 256, "magnesium": 29, "iron": 0.7, "calcium": 15, "vitaminC": 0
}

### The same food, a different lens — a different letter
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "fitness",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1.0, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Filter 2 — a lens id that does not exist — 400, pointing at GET /api/lenses
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "keto",
  "calories": 165, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Filter 1 — missing fields and a negative value — 400 with every error at once
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{ "lensId": "fitness", "calories": 165, "protein": -5 }

### Filter 3 — kilojoules sent as if they were kilocalories — 400
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "fitness",
  "calories": 690, "protein": 31, "fat": 3.6, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 1, "sodium": 74, "carbohydrate": 0
}

### Water — 200, but grade: null. No letter, not a bad letter.
POST {{host}}/api/grades
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "lensId": "weight-loss",
  "calories": 0, "protein": 0, "fat": 0, "fiber": 0,
  "saturatedFat": 0, "sodium": 4, "carbohydrate": 0
}

# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods
#
# The catalogue: 1 933 foods loaded from FNDDS. Four optional parameters,
# all of them combinable:
#
#   search    — case-insensitive "contains" on the description, run by
#               PostgreSQL as ILIKE
#   category  — exact match on the FNDDS category, as stored. Spaces must be
#               written %20: a raw space ends the URL in the request line
#   page      — 1-based. Out of range is not an error: items comes back empty
#   pageSize  — 25 by default, 100 at most. Above that: 400
#
# The response is an envelope, not a bare array: items carries the page,
# total carries how many rows match the filters before paging cuts them.
#
# A list row carries id, description and category only. The sixteen nutrients
# never leave the database for a list — the projection asks PostgreSQL for
# three columns.
#
# Ordering is by description, always. Without it PostgreSQL owes no promise
# that two requests return rows in the same order, and page 2 could repeat or
# skip what page 1 showed.
# =============================================================================

### The first page, no filters — 25 of 1 933
GET {{host}}/api/foods
Accept: application/json

### Everything with milk in the name — total must be 93
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=milk
Accept: application/json

### The same search shouted — ILIKE ignores case, total stays 93
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=MILK
Accept: application/json

### One category, exactly as stored — total must be 160
GET {{host}}/api/foods?category=Chicken,%20whole%20pieces
Accept: application/json

### Search and category together — both filters apply
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=roasted&category=Chicken,%20whole%20pieces
Accept: application/json

### Page 2 of the milks, five at a time
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=milk&page=2&pageSize=5
Accept: application/json

### Past the last page — 200 with an empty items, not 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods?page=500
Accept: application/json

### Over the ceiling — 400, the [Range] attribute stops it before the action
GET {{host}}/api/foods?pageSize=500
Accept: application/json

### Page zero — 400, pages are 1-based
GET {{host}}/api/foods?page=0
Accept: application/json
# =============================================================================
# GET /api/foods/{id}
#
# One food, with the sixteen nutrients the list leaves out. The id is the
# database key, stable for the life of the row (ADR 0006) — safe to store in a
# URL, a log entry or a foreign key.
#
# Ids in the loaded catalogue run from 5347 to 7279. They do not start at 1:
# the identity sequence kept counting through the reloads that happened before
# 0006 stopped them.
#
#   200  the food, as an object
#   404  no row with that id, AND anything that is not a number — the {id:int}
#        route constraint means /api/foods/milk matches no route at all, so it
#        never reaches the action and never becomes a 400
#
# fdcId is provenance: the USDA row these numbers came from. It is null for a
# food typed in by hand, which is not the same as "no source".
#
# A nutrient is null when the catalogue has no value for it, which is not zero.
# leucine is null on all 1 933 rows — the engine derives it from the category
# instead of reading it here.
# =============================================================================

### Whole milk by id — 200, sixteen nutrients, fdcId 2705385, leucine null
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5348
Accept: application/json

### The first row of the catalogue — id 5347, not 1
GET {{host}}/api/foods/5347
Accept: application/json

### The last row — id 7279
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7279
Accept: application/json

### One past the last id — 404, not an empty object
GET {{host}}/api/foods/7280
Accept: application/json

### An id below the range the sequence ever reached — 404
GET {{host}}/api/foods/1
Accept: application/json

### Not a number — 404 from the route constraint, before any action runs
GET {{host}}/api/foods/milk
Accept: application/json

### Find it in the list first, then open it — the id in items is this id
GET {{host}}/api/foods?search=broccoli,%20raw
Accept: application/json

### The row the search above returns — 200
GET {{host}}/api/foods/6626
Accept: application/json
